# Tannous.Pos AI Implementation Rules

Use these rules as mandatory defaults for all generated code in this repository.

## 1) Architecture and Dependencies
- Preserve layered flow: `WebApi` -> `Application` -> `Domain`; `Infrastructure` implements persistence/external services.
- Do not add dependencies that invert layer direction.
- For new backend business logic, use CQRS (`MediatR` command/query handlers) in `Application`.
- Do not introduce new architectural styles or frameworks unless already established in the touched area.

## 2) Controller / Service / Repository Responsibilities
- Keep controllers transport-focused (HTTP concerns, auth attributes, result mapping, status codes).
- Put business orchestration in `Application` handlers/services, not controllers.
- Repository interfaces belong in `Domain`; implementations belong in `Infrastructure`.
- Use `UnitOfWork`/transactions for multi-step writes requiring atomicity.

## 3) DTO and API Contract Rules
- Use DTOs at API/application boundaries; do not expose EF entities directly in API responses.
- Keep API route/versioning consistent with existing versioned conventions for new/updated endpoints.
- Keep request/response and error shapes consistent for equivalent endpoints.
- Prefer existing pagination DTO conventions where pagination is required.

## 4) Validation and Error Handling
- Use FluentValidation validators in `Application` for request/business validation.
- Keep controller validation limited to transport checks (headers/route/body format).
- Prefer centralized exception-to-response handling conventions over ad-hoc per-endpoint error shapes.
- Do not duplicate reusable header checks when shared filters/middleware already exist.

## 5) Security and Logging
- Keep JWT/auth settings in configuration/environment; never hardcode secrets.
- Use existing role/policy constants and authorization extensions; avoid inline role strings.
- Preserve rate-limiting, idempotency, and device validation patterns for write-sensitive endpoints.
- Follow Serilog + correlation-id/log enrichment conventions; do not log sensitive data.
- Keep CORS policies environment-appropriate; do not widen production exposure by default.

## 6) Async, Naming, and Code Style
- Use async end-to-end for I/O paths and suffix async methods with `Async`.
- Avoid fake async wrappers around synchronous operations in new code.
- Use existing naming conventions: `PascalCase` types/methods/properties, `camelCase` locals/params, `I*` interfaces, `*Command/*Query/*Handler/*Validator/*Dto`.
- Prefer constructor injection and focused, single-responsibility classes.
- Follow nullable reference conventions and explicit null handling used in this codebase.

## 7) Folder Placement and Change Discipline
- Place files in the matching layer/feature folder (`Domain`, `Application`, `Infrastructure`, `WebApi`, `mobile` modules).
- Keep `EF Core` model/migration changes inside Infrastructure migration flow.
- Maintain consistency with existing local mapping style in touched modules (currently mostly manual mapping).
- Prefer incremental consistency improvements over broad rewrites.

## 8) Operational Cognition Sustainability (post–Step 45)

The operational cognition stack (Steps 29–45) is complete. Future steps are **sustainability-governed**, not expansion-driven.

- **Before any step:** pass all four admission gates — Semantic, Complexity, Continuity, Operator (see `IMPROVEMENT_REPORT.md`).
- **Do not add cognition layers** without gate justification; prefer Direction A (consolidation), B (delivery surfaces), or C (historical continuity).
- **Consolidation only:** light, explicit shared primitives — no generic cognition engines, reflection, metadata-driven aggregation, or plugin systems.
- **Every step report** must include "Remaining Limitations & Architectural Pressure Assessment."
- **Hard constraints unchanged:** bounded FIFO stores, process-local, GET-only, sequential hub composition, inline aggregations, no synthesis-service recursion, advisory-only.

